# Technical Reference

This page has technical information about Lagrange's configuration files, custom file formats, and related utilities. For instructions on using the app, see the Help page.

# 1. User data

## 1.1 User directory

The location of user data files can be changed with the --user option. By default, Lagrange stores user-specific persistent files in one of the following locations (depending on the operating system):

```Location of user-specific files on different operating systems (table)
   Windows : C:\Users\Name\AppData\Roaming\fi.skyjake.Lagrange
     macOS : ~/Library/Application Support/fi.skyjake.Lagrange/
Other Unix : ~/.config/lagrange/
```

Files with the .lst extension are lists where each line is one item. The first line of the file is a header that identifies the format version:
```
list fmt:1
```
Empty lines and lines beginning with "#" are ignored.

Older versions of Lagrange used .txt files instead (for example, "visited.2.txt"). These are converted to the corresponding .lst files when first loaded, and they are left unchanged in the runtime directory. An old file is only read if the corresponding new file does not exist.

## 1.2 Files

### bindings.lst
Each line specifies one keybinding:
> {binding-id} {shortcut}
* Binding IDs are defined in src/ui/keys.c. The file has a comment above each binding showing the corresponding UI command.
* Shortcut is a key name preceded by zero or more modifiers, separated with "+". For example: "ctrl+shift+k".
* Key names are SDL key names (case-insensitive), for example "pageup" or "f5". A key can also be specified as a hexadecimal SDL keycode, for example "0x4000004b".
* The modifiers are: "shift", "ctrl", "alt", "gui" (Windows/Command/Meta key), and "caps" (Caps Lock). "primary" means Command on macOS and Ctrl on other platforms.
* "none" means that no key is bound.

The terminal version uses "cbindings.lst" instead.

### bookmarks.ini
This file stores all bookmarks and bookmark folders. The format is a subset of TOML: the only supported value types are strings and integers.
```Example bookmark
[51]
url = "gemini://geminiprotocol.net/"
title = "Project Gemini"
tags = ""
icon = 0x264a
created = 1609936844  # 2021-01-06
parent = 99
order = 288
```
* The table name (51) is the unique identifier of the bookmark.
* "url", "title", and "tags" are used as-is. C-style backslash escapes must be used for quotes (for example: \"), and may optionally be used for Unicode characters as well.
* "icon" is a UTF-32 codepoint as a hexadecimal number.
* "created" is a UNIX timestamp for when the bookmark was created. Following the timestamp, a human-readable date is also provided but it is just a comment — that part will be ignored when parsing the file.
* "parent" is the ID of the parent folder. Folders are stored as similar bookmark entries, but their URL value is always an empty string.
* "order" is for sorting the bookmarks list. The list is sorted by ascending order. This value is updated automatically when bookmarks are reordered in the sidebar.

### feeds.lgr
A binary file that contains the cached state of feed subscriptions: all the known entries and when feeds were last refreshed. The file may be deleted while the application is not running to force a reset of feed contents. Subscriptions themselves are tracked via bookmark tags so deleting the file does not affect which pages are subscribed.

### fonts.ini
This file is loaded as fontpack metadata (see section 5). It must be manually created.

### idents.lgr and idents/
idents.lgr is a binary file that contains metadata about the client certificates known to the application, for example on which URLs a particular certificate should be active.

The "idents" subdirectory is where the actual client certificates are stored. Each certificate comprises a .crt and a .key file in PEM format. Certificates generated inside the application use the certificate fingerprint as the file name.

### modmap.lst
Translation table for keyboard modifiers. For example, one could swap the Alt and Ctrl keys by changing their mappings. See the comment in the file that explains the format and usage.

### palette.txt
Palette for UI color themes. This configuration file does not exist by default and must be created manually.

The actual colors used in the user interface are derived from the base palette. Therefore, you should not change the colors too drastically, but instead follow the expected pattern: a range of 5 intensities (black-white), followed by two accent color pairs (dim, bright). The last five colors are reserved for certain specific uses, e.g., the green is used for the navbar lock icon to indicate a trusted server certificate. The palettes defined here do not affect page color themes, only the UI. Note that link icons are considered part of the UI, so you may find that changing the palette will reduce visibility of the icons with some of the page color themes.

The file specifies two palettes: one for dark mode and one for light mode. The current palette is selected with the lines:
```
# Dark
```
and
```
# Light
```

The other lines specify colors:
> {label}: {rgb-hex}
> {label}: {red} {green} {blue}
* {rgb-hex} must use the form "#RRGGBB"
* {red}, {green}, {blue} are decimal numbers in the range 0...255

The configuration below defines the built-in default palette:
```palette.txt with the default UI palette
# Dark

black:      #000000
gray25:     #282828
gray50:     #505050
gray75:     #A0A0A0
white:      #FFFFFF
brown:      106     80      0
orange:     255     192     0
teal:       0       96      128
cyan:       0       192     255
yellow:     255     255     32
red:        255     64      64
magenta:    255     0       255
blue:       132     132     255
green:      0       200     0

# Light

black:      0       0       0
gray25:     75      75      75
gray50:     150     150     150
gray75:     235     235     235
white:      255     255     255
brown:      210     120      10
orange:     235     215     200
teal:       10      110     130
cyan:       170     215     220
yellow:     255     255     32
red:        255     64      64
magenta:    255     0       255
blue:       132     132     255
green:      0       150     0
```

### prefs.cfg
Persistent configuration variables. The file is rewritten when the application is closed. Each line is interpreted as an internal UI event like those printed with the --echo command line option.

### prefs/custom.ini
Optional user-written rules for document preferences. The application never writes this file. See section 2.

### prefs/document.ini
Document preferences that apply to all protocols. Only the values that differ from the defaults are stored. Deleting this file resets document preferences to defaults.

### prefs/protocols.ini
Document preferences customized for specific protocols. Deleting this file removes all protocol-specific customization.

### prefs/sitespec.ini
Site-specific settings. In addition to document theme customization, this includes the Titan upload settings (port, identity, and token), identities previously used on the site (for quick access via the navbar Identities button), and input prompt paths (for which input popup opens automatically before requesting the path). Deleting this file forgets all of these. Note that Titan upload tokens are stored as plain text.

### state.lgr
A binary file that contains the current state of the application: open tabs and their scroll positions, and navigation history with cached page content (up to the configured cache limit). You may delete this file when the application is not running to close all tabs and clear the page content cache.

### tools.ini
Configuration of external tools, such as content filters. See section 3.

### trusted.lst
Trusted server certificates. Each line specifies the certificate fingerprint of one server:
> {domain};{port} {valid-until} {fingerprint}
* Domain names are stored in punycode format if they contain non-ASCII characters.
* The "valid until" expiration date is a UNIX timestamp.
* The fingerprint is an SHA-256 checksum of the server certificate's public key in DER format.

### uploadbackup.txt
Backup of the text entered into the Upload with Titan dialog.

### visited.lst
The combined navigation history of all tabs. Each line specifies one URL:
> {last-visited} {flags} {url}
* The last visited time is stored as a UNIX timestamp.
* Flags is always four bytes long:
```URL flags (table)
0000 : No flags set
0001 : Transient
0002 : Kept
```
The Transient flag is used to indicate redirections and for marking feed entries as read without actually visiting the URL. URLs with the Transient flag do not appear in the History sidebar tab.

The Kept flag is used for preventing the URL from being discarded due to old age. Normally URLs in the navigation history become stale after several months and are removed from the file.

# 2. Custom document preferences

Document preferences can be set for URLs and media types matching regular expressions using the file "prefs/custom.ini" in the user directory (see section 1.1). This complements the global, site-specific, and protocol-specific settings that are edited in the app.

Lagrange never writes "prefs/custom.ini". The file is only checked at launch, so the app must be restarted for changes to take effect.

The file uses TOML syntax. Each `[[scope]]` table defines one rule. Tables with other names are ignored.

```custom.ini
[[scope]]
mime        = "^text/markdown"
order       = 50
markdownAsSource = false

[[scope]]
uri         = '^gemini://example\.com/fiction/'
order       = 150
lineWidth   = 32
italicQuote = false
```

When a page is opened, the rules that match it are checked in order of precedence. The first rule that defines a property determines its value. Properties not defined by any rule use the built-in defaults.

## 2.1 Rule properties

* `uri` (string): Regular expression that is matched against the page URL, ignoring case.
* `mime` (string): Regular expression that is matched against the media type of the page, ignoring case.
* `matchMode` (string): "both" = all the given patterns must match. "either" = the rule applies when either of the patterns matches. Defaults to "both".
* `order` (integer): Precedence of the rule. Rules with lower values take precedence. This is required and must be in the range 0...899; other rules are ignored.

A rule with neither `uri` nor `mime` applies to all pages. A malformed regular expression never matches. Single-quoted strings are convenient for regular expressions because backslashes are not treated as escape characters.

The settings edited in the app have fixed orders:
```Orders of built-in scopes (table)
100 : Site-specific settings
500 : Protocol-specific settings
900 : "All protocols" settings
```
A built-in scope takes precedence over a custom rule that has the same order. Custom rules with the same order are checked in the order they appear in the file.

## 2.2 Document properties

The keys and values are the same as in "prefs/document.ini", "prefs/protocols.ini", and "prefs/sitespec.ini". Booleans can also be given as integers (0 or 1). Out-of-range values are clamped to the valid range.

### 2.2.1 Typography
* `headingFont` (string): ID of the typeface used for headings. Typeface IDs are defined in fontpacks (see section 5). Defaults to "default".
* `bodyFont` (string): ID of the typeface used for body text. Defaults to "default".
* `monospaceFont` (string): ID of the typeface used for preformatted blocks. Defaults to "iosevka".
* `monospaceDocumentFont` (string): ID of the typeface used for body text when `monospaceDocument` is enabled. Defaults to "iosevka-body".
* `monospaceDocument` (bool): Use a monospace typeface for body text. Defaults to false.

### 2.2.2 Layout
* `lineWidth` (integer): Width of the text column as a multiple of the font size. At least 20. A value of 1000 or more fills the available width. Defaults to 38 (1000 on phones).
* `lineSpacing` (float): Multiplier for line spacing. At least 0.5. Defaults to 1.0.
* `tabWidth` (integer): Number of spaces between tab stops. At least 1. Defaults to 8.
* `plainTextWrap` (bool): Wrap long lines in plain text documents. Defaults to true.
* `expandToLongLines` (bool): Let the text column expand up to the window width when the content has long lines that cannot be wrapped. Defaults to true.
* `centerShortDocs` (bool): Center documents vertically when they are shorter than the window. Defaults to true.
* `pageView` (bool): Show the document as pages instead of a scrolling view. Defaults to false.
* `collapsePre` (integer): When preformatted blocks are collapsed: 0 (never), 1 (manually), 2 (at page load), 3 (always). Defaults to 1.

### 2.2.3 Style
* `justify` (integer): When paragraphs are justified: 0 (never), 1 (in the paginated view only), 2 (always). Defaults to 1.
* `hyphenate` (bool): Hyphenate words when wrapping lines. Defaults to false.
* `bigFirstParagraph` (bool): Show the first paragraph in a larger font. Defaults to true.
* `italicQuote` (bool): Show quote text in italics. Defaults to true.
* `quoteIcon` (bool): Indicate quotes with a quote icon; if false, with a vertical line. Defaults to true.
* `sideIcon` (bool): Show the capsule icon on the left side of the page. Defaults to true.
* `boldLinkVisited` (bool): Use bold text also for visited links. Defaults to false.
* `boldLinkDark` (bool): Use bold text for links on dark backgrounds. Defaults to false.
* `boldLinkLight` (bool): Use bold text for links on light backgrounds. Defaults to true.

### 2.2.4 Theme
* `docThemeDark` (integer): Page color theme in dark mode. Defaults to 0.
* `docThemeLight` (integer): Page color theme in light mode. Defaults to 4.
* `pageThemeDark` (integer): Page color theme in dark mode when using the paginated view. -1 means the same as `docThemeDark`. Defaults to 3.
* `pageThemeLight` (integer): Page color theme in light mode when using the paginated view. -1 means the same as `docThemeLight`. Defaults to 9.
* `saturation` (float): Color saturation of the page theme, in the range 0.0...1.0. Defaults to 1.0.
* `imageStyle` (integer): How images are colorized: 0 (none), 1 (grayscale), 2 (background to foreground), 3 (text color), 4 (preformatted color). Defaults to 0.

```Page color themes (table)
 0 : Colorful Dark
 1 : Colorful Light
 2 : Black
 3 : Gray
 4 : White
 5 : Sepia
 6 : High Contrast
 7 : Oceanic
 8 : Vibrant Light
 9 : Paper
10 : Arboreal
11 : Ember
```

### 2.2.5 Content
* `ansiEscapes` (integer): Which ANSI escape sequences are applied in Gemtext, as a sum of flags: 1 (foreground color), 2 (background color), 4 (font style). 0 means all escapes are ignored. Defaults to 1.
* `geminiStyledGopher` (bool): Autodetect when Gopher menus can be styled like Gemtext. Defaults to false.
* `markdownAsSource` (bool): Show Markdown documents as source text instead of rendering them. Defaults to true.

### 2.2.6 Other
* `paletteSeed` (string): Text used for generating the page theme colors instead of the URL's host name. Empty means that the host name is used. Has no effect on local files.
* `zoomPercent` (integer): Zoom level as a percentage, in the range 50...200. Defaults to 100.

# 3. Content filters

Content filters enable piping Gemini responses through external programs for arbitrary processing. This allows leveraging scripting languages and binary executables to perform format conversions, content transformation, and data analysis.

Filters are configured using the file "tools.ini" in Lagrange's config directory. Each filter has a regular expression that is matched against the response MIME type and parameters, and each matching filter is offered the response body via stdin. The called external programs are free to rewrite the entire response, including the MIME type. If one of the filters returns a valid response, it is used as the final response of the Gemini request.

Example use cases:

* Parsing an Atom XML feed and generating a Gemini feed index page
* Rendering a MIDI file using Timidity as PCM WAV
* Generating a graph PNG based on values in a CSV
* Converting HTML or Markdown to 'text/gemini'
* Language translations

Earlier versions of Lagrange used a file called "mimehooks.txt" for configuring filters (then called "MIME hooks"). It is no longer used; any existing hooks must be manually rewritten in tools.ini.

There is a built-in "Unwrap Paragraphs" tool for all "text/*" pages. It joins hard-wrapped lines into paragraphs, guessing which line breaks were intentional.

## 3.1 Interface

The response body is provided to the filter program via stdin. The program is run with a minimal CGI-like environment:
* CONTENT_TYPE is the MIME type and parameters of the response, for example "text/gemini; lang=ja".
* REQUEST_URL is the URL of the request.

The rest of the environment variables are inherited from Lagrange.

Output from the program (via stdout) must be a valid "20" Gemini response that includes the new MIME type:
```
20 text/gemini; lang=en\r\n
{...body...}
```

Any output that does not follow this format is considered to mean that the filter refused to process the contents. The next available filter will be offered the response instead.

## 3.2 tools.ini syntax

tools.ini uses TOML syntax. Each tool is defined as a table whose name is a unique ID for the tool. Lagrange must be restarted for changes to the configuration file to take effect.

For example:
```tools.ini
[atom2gmi]
name    = "Convert Atom to Gemini feed"
command = ["/usr/bin/python3", "/home/jaakko/atomconv.py"]
mime    = '^application/(atom\+)?xml'
```

The following properties are supported:
* `name` (string): Human-readable label for reporting to the user. Defaults to the tool ID.
* `command` (array of strings): The program to execute, followed by its arguments. The program is executed directly without involving the shell. This means scripts must be invoked via the interpreter executable. A program name without a directory is searched for in PATH.
* `mime` (string): Regular expression that is matched against the MIME type and parameters, ignoring case. If omitted, the tool applies to all content.
* `enabled` (boolean): Set to false to disable the tool without removing its definition. Defaults to true.
* `auto` (boolean): Whether the tool is used as a content filter, i.e., run automatically on matching responses. Set to false for tools that are only run manually. Defaults to true.
* `menu` (boolean): Show the tool in the "Tools" submenu of the page context menu. Defaults to false.
* `key` (string): Keyboard shortcut for running the tool on the current page, for example "ctrl+shift+t". The syntax is the same as in "bindings.lst" (see section 1.2). Tool shortcuts take precedence over other key bindings, and they are not shown in Preferences.

Filters are tried in the order they are defined in the file. Lagrange's built-in filters (e.g., for Atom feeds) are tried after the configured ones.

Single-quoted strings are literal, i.e., backslashes are not treated as escape characters. This is convenient for regular expressions and Windows paths. In double-quoted strings, backslashes must be escaped ("\\").

On macOS, apps launched via Finder or the Dock are given a minimal PATH that does not include, for example, Homebrew's installation directory. Using absolute program paths is therefore recommended.

Errors in tools.ini are reported at launch, and the details are shown in "about:debug".

## 3.3 Running tools manually

A tool can also be run manually on the current page via the context menu or a keyboard shortcut. The tool is only available when the page's MIME type matches the tool's `mime` regexp. The page source is passed to the tool like for content filters (see 4.1), and the tool runs in the background. When the tool finishes, its output replaces the page contents, possibly changing the MIME type. Reloading the page restores the original contents.

For example, a tool that is not used as a content filter but appears in the context menu of Gemtext pages:
```tools.ini
[translate]
name    = "Translate to English"
command = ["/usr/bin/python3", "/home/jaakko/translate.py", "en"]
mime    = '^text/gemini'
auto    = false
menu    = true
key     = "primary+shift+e"
```

## 3.4 Example: Converting from Atom to Gemini

The following simple Python script demonstrates how a content filter could be used to parse an Atom XML document using Python 3 and output a Gemini feed index page based on the parsed entries. This is just a simple example; a more robust script could include more content from the Atom feed and handle errors, too.
```python
import sys
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

def atomtag(n):
    return '{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}' + n

root = ET.fromstring(sys.stdin.read())
if root.tag != atomtag('feed'):
    sys.exit(0)
feed_title = ''
feed_author = ''
feed_entries = []
for child in root:
    if child.tag == atomtag('title'):
        feed_title = child.text
    elif child.tag == atomtag('entry'):
        feed_entries.append(child)
print("20 text/gemini\r")
print(f'# {feed_title}')
for entry in feed_entries:
    entry_date = ''
    entry_title = ''
    entry_link = ''
    for child in entry:
        if child.tag == atomtag('updated'):
            entry_date = child.text[:10]
        elif child.tag == atomtag('title'):
            entry_title = child.text
        elif child.tag == atomtag('link'):
            entry_link = child.attrib['href']
    print(f'=> {entry_link} {entry_date} {entry_title}')
```

# 4. User data archives

Lagrange user data archives are compressed ZIP archives that contain browsing history, identities, bookmarks, text snippets, site-specific settings, and/or trusted server certificate fingerprints.

An export archive largely mirrors the contents of the runtime directory (section 1). However, the "lagrange-export.ini" metadata file must also be present:

```lagrange-export.ini
# Lagrange user data exported on 2022-04-01 13:34
version = "1.12.0"
timestamp = 1648809264
```
* `version` (string): Version of the app that generated the archive.
* `timestamp` (integer): Time of generation as a UNIX timestamp.

# 5. Fontpacks

Fontpack is a file format defined by Lagrange that is used to combine a set of font files with configuration metadata. The (unregistered) media type for .fontpack files is 'application/lagrange-fontpack+zip'.

A fontpack is a ZIP archive that must have a "fontpack.ini" file at the root level.

```fontpack.ini from firasans.fontpack
version = 1

[firasans]
name        = "Fira Sans"
glyphscale  = 0.85
regular     = "FiraSans-Regular.ttf"
italic      = "FiraSans-Italic.ttf"
light       = "FiraSans-Light.ttf"
semibold    = "FiraSans-SemiBold.ttf"
bold        = "FiraSans-Bold.ttf"
```

Fontpacks may use the Deflate compression method, but that can slow down launching the app because each font must then be decompressed before loading. Therefore, it is recommended to use the -0 (--compression-method store) option when creating the archive. You may also find the -j (--junk-paths) option helpful if the current working directory is not the one where fontpack.ini is located.

Each fontpack has an ID that comes from the file name: the ID of "firasans.fontpack" would be "firasans". Space characters are not allowed in the ID.

Fontpacks are versioned, i.e., they have a version number in their metadata. This makes it possible to tell when a fontpack is an updated version of an existing one. The fontpack ID (file name) must remain the same, though, regardless of the version number.

The "fontpack.ini" file uses TOML syntax and can specify any number of typefaces. In the example above, the line `[firasans]` begins the definition of a typeface called "firasans". If the same typeface is found in multiple fontpacks, only the first one that gets loaded is used; the rest are ignored. Typeface IDs can be the same or different than fontpack IDs; they exist in different namespaces. The typeface ID is what is actually used in "prefs.cfg" to save your font preferences.

## 5.1 Fontpack properties

`version` defines the version number of the fontpack. It is a single integer, and if omitted, defaults to zero. It has no specific meaning except that larger values are considered newer versions.

## 5.2 Typeface properties

### 5.2.1 General
* `name` (string) is the human-readable name of the typeface. This is used in the Preferences dialog and elsewhere in the UI.

### 5.2.2 Prioritization and exclusion
The same character's glyph(s) may be found in several fonts, so a prioritization is applied to pick the most appropriate font. Font selection may also depend on the context, for example preformatted blocks must be displayed using monospace fonts. These properties control when and if the typeface will be used.

* `priority` (integer) defines a sort order for typefaces. The ones with the highest priority are checked first.
* `override` (bool): whether this typeface is always checked first before any other one, in any context. Only one typeface can use this flag.
* `monospace` (bool): is this a monospace typeface and therefore suitable for preformatted blocks and monospace body text.
* `auxiliary` (bool): if true, this typeface is not included in Preferences as a font choice, but is still checked for glyphs if other fonts do not provide them. Suitable for miscellaneous symbols or additional languages, for examples.
* `allowspace` (bool): if true, an auxiliary font's metrics for space characters are used. Sometimes symbol fonts have extra wide spaces, so for example a space next to an Emoji would be too wide. This has no effect for non-auxiliary typefaces.

### 5.2.3 Metrics
These properties can be used to modify the height and spacing of the font.

* `height` (float) is a multiplier to alter the height of the font. It can be at most 2.0, to double the height. This simply scales the font smaller or larger without altering spacing.
* `glyphscale` (float) scales glyphs smaller without changing the height of the font. The value must be 1.0 or smaller. In effect, this lets you increase the spacing between consecutive lines of text. It can be combined with `height` to keep glyphs the same size but just increase line spacing. For example, `glyphscale` 0.833 would counter the scaling caused by `height` 1.2, increasing line spacing by 1.2x.
* `voffset` (float) is multiplier applied to the vertical offset that normally centers scaled glyphs on the line. This allows fine-tuning where the baseline of scaled glyphs ends up. Setting this to 1.0 means that glyphs are vertically centered after scaling with `glyphscale`; 0.0 would disable the centering. Reasonable values are likely in the 0.5…1.5 range, depending on the amount of scaling.

Changing only the `glyphscale` property is the recommended way to alter line spacing. This makes it easier to mix and match fonts on a single line without having to deal with changes in text height. Compare your adjusted scaling to see if it looks suitable next to normal body text.

Each of these properties can be set separately for UI text and page content by using the prefixes `ui.` and `doc.`. For example, to only scale the font when used in the UI, set `ui.height` instead of `height`.

### 5.2.4 Font files
A single typeface can use up to five font files.

* `regular` (string): path of a font file for the regular style. A relative path means that the file is loaded from inside the fontpack archive. Absolute paths can be used to refer to files on the local system.
* `italic` (string): path of a font file for the italic style.
* `light` (string): path of a font file for the light weight.
* `semibold` (string): path of a font file for the semibold weight.
* `bold` (string): path of a font file for the bold weight.

Only the regular style is required. Undefined styles fall back to the closest available style. The same file path can be used in multiple styles; only one copy of the file will be loaded to memory.

When the path refers to a TrueType Collection (.ttc), the font index to use must be appended to the file name:
```.ttc example
regular = "NotoSansCJK-Regular.ttc:2"
```

# 6. Gempub

Gempub is an e-book format for Gemtext content. A Gempub book is a ZIP archive with the extension ".gpub" and the media type 'application/gpub+zip'. It contains Gemtext files, an index page that lists the chapters, supporting files like images, and a "metadata.txt" file.

=> https://codeberg.org/oppenlab/gempub  Gempub specification

## 6.1 Requirements

* All text content must be Gemtext, encoded in UTF-8. Images can be PNG or JPEG.
* The index page is the table of contents. By default, it is "index.gmi" but the book metadata may specify a different path.
* The reading order of the chapters is the order of the local links on the index page. Remote links (with a scheme, like "gemini://") are not chapters, and linked chapters are not searched for further links.
* Links between files in the book are relative paths inside the archive.

Lagrange only recognizes an archive as a Gempub book if it has a "metadata.txt" at the root. Otherwise, it is viewed as a plain ZIP archive.

## 6.2 metadata.txt

Each line has a key and a value, separated by a colon. Whitespace around the value is ignored, and keys can be in any order. Unknown keys are ignored.

```metadata.txt example
gpubVersion: 1.0.0
title: An Example Book
author: Jane Doe
language: en
publishDate: 2026-01-31
cover: images/cover.png
```

Required keys:
* `gpubVersion`: Version of the Gempub format, "1.0.0".
* `title`: Title of the book.

Optional keys:
* `author`: Author of the book.
* `charset`: Character set of the text. Defaults to UTF-8. Lagrange only supports UTF-8.
* `copyright`: Copyright notice.
* `cover`: Path of the cover image (JPEG or PNG). If omitted, Lagrange uses "cover.jpg" or "cover.png" at the root of the archive, if present.
* `description`: Short description of the book.
* `index`: Path of the index page. Defaults to "index.gmi".
* `language`: Language of the book as a BCP 47 code (for example, "en" or "fi").
* `license`: License of the book.
* `publishDate`: Date of publication (YYYY-MM-DD).
* `published`: Year of publication (YYYY), when the exact date is not known.
* `revisionDate`: Date of the latest revision (YYYY-MM-DD).
* `version`: Human-readable version of the book. It is not meant to be parsed.

## 6.3 Books in Lagrange

The following implementation details should be noted:

* A book on the bookshelf is identified by its title and author. A book with the same title and author is considered to be a copy of the same book.
* `publishDate` (or `published`) determines the edition of the book. When a book is opened, a later date than the one on the bookshelf means it is a newer edition.
* The book's cover page is generated by Lagrange from the metadata: the cover image, title, author, description, version, dates, language, license, and copyright. The index page is shown as the book's table of contents.

# 7. gmget

The Core library comes with `gmget`, a simple curl-like utility for making network requests on the command line. It is a thin command line interface on top of the same code that Lagrange uses for requests, so it can be useful in scripts that need access to Lagrange's identities and other user data.

`gmget` is built from the "lib/core" directory of the Lagrange source tree. It is not included in the app packages.

```gmget --help
Usage: gmget [OPTIONS] URL...

Options:
  -o FILE, --output=FILE  Write response body to FILE instead of stdout
  -I IDENT, --ident=IDENT Use the Lagrange identity matching IDENT
  -U DIR, --user=DIR      User data directory (default: platform-specific)
  -p, --print             Print response status and meta string to stderr
  -h, --help              Print this help and exit
```

* URLs without a scheme are assumed to be "gemini://" URLs. When multiple URLs are given, the response bodies are written one after another.
* IDENT can be a partial client certificate fingerprint or common name, ignoring case.
* Status and meta are always printed to stderr if the request fails.
* Identities and trusted server certificates are read from the user directory, but gmget never modifies them.
* Content filters (see section 3) are not applied.

# 8. Self-hosted sync server

Lagrange syncs user data via a sync server. By default, the public server at sync.skyjake.fi:1966 is used, but you can also run your own with `lgrsyncd` (Lagrange Sync Daemon).

`lgrsyncd` can be found in the "sync" directory of the Lagrange source tree. It can be compiled separately from the app.

```Building lgrsyncd
cmake -S sync -B build-sync
cmake --build build-sync
```

## 8.1 Running the server

```lgrsyncd --help
Usage: lgrsyncd [options]

  -d, --dir DIR        Data directory (default: current directory)
  -p, --port N         Listening port (default: 1965)
  -h, --host NAME      Host name for a generated server certificate
      --cert FILE      Server certificate (PEM, Ed25519); generated if missing
      --key FILE       Server private key (PEM); generated if missing
      --max-size N     Max stored data per account in bytes (default: 1048576)
      --max-accounts N Max number of accounts; 0 for no limit (default: 5000)
      --expire-days N  Remove accounts inactive for N days; 0 to keep forever
                       (default: 365)
      --max-per-ip N   Max simultaneous connections per IP address; 0 for no
                       limit (default: 4)
      --allow FILE     Allowed client certificate fingerprints (SHA-256, hex),
                       one per line; if omitted, anyone with an Ed25519
                       certificate can sync
  -v, --verbose        Log all requests (default: only errors and daily stats)
```

* If the certificate and key files are missing, a self-signed Ed25519 certificate is generated for the given host name and saved in the data directory as "server.crt" and "server.key".
* Account data is stored in the "accounts" subdirectory of the data directory.
* The allow list file has one fingerprint per line. Empty lines and lines beginning with "#" are ignored.

For example:
```
lgrsyncd \
    --dir ~/.local/share/lgrsyncd \
    --port 1966 \
    --host sync.example.com
```

On Linux, the server can be run as a systemd user service. See "sync/lgrsyncd.service.example" for a template.
